KG42.register("nodes-ed419586f5f8a5955a280ead",{"id":"COMP-29875534ea059f1d","code":"SET028","label":"Resource purchase and supply revenue-cost accounting and netting","definition":"Account for participants' resource purchase costs and supply revenues; by source, retain the composition of production cost, transport cost, or demand value, forming a charge, a revenue, or a net amount, respectively.","layer":"L8","template_code":"JOC","concept_id":"settlement.payment","concept_ids":["settlement.payment"],"identity_dimensions":{"identity_fields":["Economic purpose: account for the receipts and payments formed by resource transactions","Primary objects: purchase costs, supply revenues, and source-specified net amounts","Responsibility relation: the receipt-and-payment relation of a participant as resource buyer and as supplier"]},"exclusions":[["Case 6 equilibrium point: C_i^{(l)}(λ^*)=C_i(E_i^{(c)}+ε_i^{(s)}−∑_{j∈S_*}E_{j,i})+∑_{j∈S_*}γ(E_{j,i})+∑_{j∈S_*}λ_j^*E_{j,i}−λ_i^*ε_i^{(s)}, using the corresponding locally optimal quantities.","Electricity purchases are paid at the supplier price λ_j^*; electricity sales are reckoned as revenue at the participant's own price λ_i^*.","γ is the transport-cost function shared by each connection and may include a transaction tax of the distribution-network operator; that tax is not charged again separately.","Generation cost is an economic-accounting item and is not specified as a payment to a counterparty."],["The electricity charge for the total cleared requested quantity is π_{n_h}d; the reference node is the hub n_h.","The consumer concurrently serves as the virtual-link service provider; the migration remuneration is ∑_{n∈N_d}(π_{n_h}−π_n)δ_{n_h,n}, and the signed price difference is retained.","Total profit is the demand value α^d d minus the electricity charge plus the migration remuneration; demand value is not treated as a monetary receipt."],["The electricity charge of consumer j is π_{n_h(j)}d_j, using that consumer's own hub n_h(j).","The revenue of each virtual link is (π_snd(v)−π_rec(v))δ_v, aggregated over the V_j owned by the consumer.","Unadjusted nodal prices π_n are used, and the sign of the send-minus-receive price difference is retained."]],"members":[{"id":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R012::V::settlement.payment","rule_id":"v141-c-R012","market_meaning":"Microgrids pay for energy purchases and transportation of purchased energy, and receive payment for energy sold. Scope: Economic interpretation of energy transactions among interconnected islanded microgrids. Inter-microgrid energy transactions may incur a tax charged by the distribution network operator. Scope: Optional transaction-tax component of inter-microgrid energy-transfer costs. Condition: When the transfer-cost model includes a tax charged by the distribution network operator for energy transactions.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"settlement","context_text":"Economic interpretation of energy transactions among interconnected islanded microgrids. | Optional transaction-tax component of inter-microgrid energy-transfer costs.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g07-e007","v141-b-g23-e023"]},"constraint_refs":["27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R012::v141-c-R012-C001","27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R012::v141-c-R012-C002","27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R012::v141-c-R012-C003"],"decision_anchor":"settlement.payment","source_rule_sha256":"b3c542594673edd16b09ca916a57fa47c3a9e87bf58c053c8eb28533aa42c605","paper":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading","rule_ref":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R012","node_id":"settlement.payment","function":"settlement.payment","unit_id":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":true},{"id":"42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-c-R012::V::settlement.payment","rule_id":"v141-c-R012","market_meaning":"The consumer's load charge uses the hub price, while its virtual-link service is remunerated by the hub-minus-alternative-node price difference multiplied by shifted load. Scope: Hub-based virtual-link market for a flexible consumer offering alternative delivery nodes.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"settlement","context_text":"Hub-based virtual-link market for a flexible consumer offering alternative delivery nodes.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e024"]},"constraint_refs":["42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-c-R012::v141-c-R012-C001","42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-c-R012::v141-c-R012-C002"],"decision_anchor":"settlement.payment","source_rule_sha256":"54fa3cfd25ff88537520b3c5b754f1d4f3c6a3c7e60306a9b5451e93a0071809","paper":"42-data_center_space_time_flexibility","rule_ref":"42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-c-R012","node_id":"settlement.payment","function":"settlement.payment","unit_id":"42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-a-M03::settlement.payment","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M03","anchor":true},{"id":"42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-c-R014::V::settlement.payment","rule_id":"v141-c-R014","market_meaning":"Each consumer is charged the electricity price at its hub node and remunerated for shifting services; virtual links are paid using the price difference between sending and receiving nodes. Scope: General spatial virtual-link market; consumers and their virtual-link shifting services.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"settlement","context_text":"General spatial virtual-link market; consumers and their virtual-link shifting services.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e031"]},"constraint_refs":["42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-c-R014::v141-c-R014-C001","42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-c-R014::v141-c-R014-C002","42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-c-R014::v141-c-R014-C003","42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-c-R014::v141-c-R014-C004"],"decision_anchor":"settlement.payment","source_rule_sha256":"54e8851d9b2167717950318c62233deb81e9104139ab7ff1e7e83a5b24c64f39","paper":"42-data_center_space_time_flexibility","rule_ref":"42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-c-R014","node_id":"settlement.payment","function":"settlement.payment","unit_id":"42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-a-M04::settlement.payment","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M04","anchor":true}],"paper_ids":["27-multiple_microgrid_trading","42-data_center_space_time_flexibility"],"evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g23-e023","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III-A. Decentralizing the Problem","anchor_text":", g ining interpretation: Each Lagrange multiplier λi may be under- costood as the price per energy ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Each Lagrange multiplier λ_i may be understood as the price per energy unit requested by μG-i to sell energy to its neighbors. Then, Lagrangian function (7) can be seen as the “net expenditure” (the opposite of the net income) for μG-i, i.e., each μG pays for producing energy, for buying energy, and for transporting the energy it buys. Conversely, the μG is paid for the energy it sells. By solving problem (6), μG-i is thus maximizing its benefit for some given selling (λ_i[k]) and buying (λ_j[k], j≠i) prices per energy unit.","paper":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading","rule_id":"v141-c-R012","quote":"Each Lagrange multiplier λ_i may be understood as the price per energy unit requested by μG-i to sell energy to its neighbors. Then, Lagrangian function (7) can be seen as the “net expenditure” (the opposite of the net income) for μG-i, i.e., each μG pays for producing energy, for buying energy, and for transporting the energy it buys. Conversely, the μG is paid for the energy it sells. By solving problem (6), μG-i is thus maximizing its benefit for some given selling (λ_i[k]) and buying (λ_j[k], j≠i) prices per energy unit."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g07-e007","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p02-c2r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. System Model","anchor_text":"0 MWh for all i, j = 1, . . . , M. ach Next, let Ci(E(g)i ) and γ(Ei,j) be the costs of producing E(","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Next, let C_i(E_i^{(g)}) and γ(E_{i,j}) be the costs of producing E_i^{(g)} units of energy at μG-i and transferring E_{i,j} units of energy on the μG-i−μG-j link, respectively. This transferring cost function may model several factors. For example, the distribution network operator, as the enabler of the energy transfer between the μGs, may charge a tax for energy transactions. In addition, this transfer cost function may also account for line congestions by introducing soft constraints on the maximum capacity of the line (see Section II-A for further information). Although the solution in the following may be extended to the case where different links have different transfer cost functions, we assume that γ(·) is common among all the links in order to avoid further complexity. As it will be clearer later, our approach is quite general and only requires that all cost functions (both production and transport) satisfy some mild convexity constraints, which are summarized in Section II-A.","paper":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading","rule_id":"v141-c-R012","quote":"Next, let C_i(E_i^{(g)}) and γ(E_{i,j}) be the costs of producing E_i^{(g)} units of energy at μG-i and transferring E_{i,j} units of energy on the μG-i−μG-j link, respectively. This transferring cost function may model several factors. For example, the distribution network operator, as the enabler of the energy transfer between the μGs, may charge a tax for energy transactions. In addition, this transfer cost function may also account for line congestions by introducing soft constraints on the maximum capacity of the line (see Section II-A for further information). Although the solution in the following may be extended to the case where different links have different transfer cost functions, we assume that γ(·) is common among all the links in order to avoid further complexity. As it will be clearer later, our approach is quite general and only requires that all cost functions (both production and transport) satisfy some mild convexity constraints, which are summarized in Section II-A."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e022","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p06-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"III-C. Interpretation and Summary","anchor_text":"diture Ci (λ ) in (7), where λ stands for the optimal point of (5). For the sake of brevity, we prov","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"In Case 6, the net expenditure reads\nC^{(l)}_i(\\lambda^*) = C_i\\left(E^{(c)}_i + \\varepsilon^{(s)}_i - \\sum_{j \\in S_*} E_{j,i}\\right) + \\sum_{j \\in S_*} \\gamma(E_{j,i}) + \\sum_{j \\in S_*} \\lambda^*_j E_{j,i} - \\lambda^*_i \\varepsilon^{(s)}_i\nwhere (\\varepsilon^{(s)}_i, \\{E_{j,i}\\}) = (\\varepsilon^{(s)}_i(\\lambda^*), \\{E_{j,i}(\\lambda^*)\\}) is now the min-imum point of (6) for \\lambda = \\lambda^*.","paper":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading","rule_id":"v141-c-R012","quote":"In Case 6, the net expenditure reads\nC^{(l)}_i(\\lambda^*) = C_i\\left(E^{(c)}_i + \\varepsilon^{(s)}_i - \\sum_{j \\in S_*} E_{j,i}\\right) + \\sum_{j \\in S_*} \\gamma(E_{j,i}) + \\sum_{j \\in S_*} \\lambda^*_j E_{j,i} - \\lambda^*_i \\varepsilon^{(s)}_i\nwhere (\\varepsilon^{(s)}_i, \\{E_{j,i}\\}) = (\\varepsilon^{(s)}_i(\\lambda^*), \\{E_{j,i}(\\lambda^*)\\}) is now the min-imum point of (6) for \\lambda = \\lambda^*."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e024","location":{"location_id":"src-1c03246cb9-p07-c1r4__v141-a-M03-pricing","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"2.4 Basic formulation with virtual links","anchor_text":"∑ 𝑑− 𝛿𝑛ℎ,𝑛≥0 (2.25d) a 𝑛∈𝑑 ce 𝑓𝑙+ −𝑓𝑙−= 𝐵𝑙(𝜃snd(𝑙) −𝜃rec(𝑙)), 𝑙∈ (2.25e) pr to 𝑝∈𝑝, 𝜃∈𝜃 (2.25f) ","equation_id":"2.26","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The Lagrange function (2.24) reveals that virtual links are a service offered by the consumer. The market remunerates the consumer for the provision of this service via the profit \\sum_{n\\in N_d}(\\pi_{n_h}-\\pi_n)\\delta_{n_h,n}. This highlights that load-shifting is incentivized whenever there is a nodal price \\pi_n that is lower than the price at the hub node \\pi_{n_h}. This is analogous to how transmission is remunerated (based on nodal price differences). The market also charges the consumer via the profit (\\alpha^d-\\pi_{n_h})d; consequently, the flexible consumer acts as a prosumer and has total profit: (\\alpha^d-\\pi_{n_h})d+\\sum_{n\\in N_d}(\\pi_{n_h}-\\pi_n)\\delta_{n_h,n}=(\\alpha^d-\\pi_{n_h})\\left(d-\\sum_{n\\in N_d}\\delta_{n_h,n}\\right)+\\sum_{n\\in N_d}(\\alpha^d-\\pi_n)\\delta_{n_h,n} (2.26).","paper":"42-data_center_space_time_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R012","quote":"The Lagrange function (2.24) reveals that virtual links are a service offered by the consumer. The market remunerates the consumer for the provision of this service via the profit \\sum_{n\\in N_d}(\\pi_{n_h}-\\pi_n)\\delta_{n_h,n}. This highlights that load-shifting is incentivized whenever there is a nodal price \\pi_n that is lower than the price at the hub node \\pi_{n_h}. This is analogous to how transmission is remunerated (based on nodal price differences). The market also charges the consumer via the profit (\\alpha^d-\\pi_{n_h})d; consequently, the flexible consumer acts as a prosumer and has total profit: (\\alpha^d-\\pi_{n_h})d+\\sum_{n\\in N_d}(\\pi_{n_h}-\\pi_n)\\delta_{n_h,n}=(\\alpha^d-\\pi_{n_h})\\left(d-\\sum_{n\\in N_d}\\delta_{n_h,n}\\right)+\\sum_{n\\in N_d}(\\alpha^d-\\pi_n)\\delta_{n_h,n} (2.26)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e031","location":{"location_id":"src-1c03246cb9-p08-c1r3__v141-a-M04-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"2.5 Basic formulation with general virtual links (spatial)","anchor_text":"(e.g., data transfer costs). In this market, each consumer 𝑗∈is charged with the electricity price ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"where the set C_{\\delta}:=\\{\\delta | \\delta_v\\in[0,\\bar\\delta_v] \\forall v\\in V\\} captures the capacity constraints for virtual links. Comparing this formulation with the previous formulation (2.23), we observe that the social surplus (2.29a) now captures the operational cost of virtual links; moreover, the balance constraints (2.29b) now include spatial virtual shifts. The dual variables associated with the computing capacity constraints (2.29d) are denoted \\omega^{l}_n and \\omega^{u}_n, respectively. Note that the operational cost of virtual links resembles that of operational costs of physical transmission and, as the name suggest, these capture costs associated with load shifting (e.g., data transfer costs). In this market, each consumer j\\in D is charged with the electricity price at the hub node and also remunerated by the shifting service provided through virtual links. As shown in Section 2.4, the market pays off virtual links by the price difference between the sending and receiving nodes.","paper":"42-data_center_space_time_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R014","quote":"where the set C_{\\delta}:=\\{\\delta | \\delta_v\\in[0,\\bar\\delta_v] \\forall v\\in V\\} captures the capacity constraints for virtual links. Comparing this formulation with the previous formulation (2.23), we observe that the social surplus (2.29a) now captures the operational cost of virtual links; moreover, the balance constraints (2.29b) now include spatial virtual shifts. The dual variables associated with the computing capacity constraints (2.29d) are denoted \\omega^{l}_n and \\omega^{u}_n, respectively. Note that the operational cost of virtual links resembles that of operational costs of physical transmission and, as the name suggest, these capture costs associated with load shifting (e.g., data transfer costs). In this market, each consumer j\\in D is charged with the electricity price at the hub node and also remunerated by the shifting service provided through virtual links. As shown in Section 2.4, the market pays off virtual links by the price difference between the sending and receiving nodes."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e030","location":{"location_id":"src-1c03246cb9-p08-c1r2__v141-a-M04-clearing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"2.5 Basic formulation with general virtual links (spatial)","anchor_text":"∑ ∑ + 𝑑𝑗+ 𝛿𝑣, (𝜋𝑛) 𝑛∈ (2.29b) m 𝑗∈𝑛 𝑣∈rec𝑛 𝑑, 𝑓𝑙+ −𝑓𝑙−= 𝐵𝑙(𝜃snd(𝑙) −𝜃rec(𝑙)), 𝑙∈ (2.29c) s. ∑ ∑ ","equation_id":"2.29","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The market clearing problem with spatial virtual links is: min_{d,p,f,\\theta,\\delta}\\sum_{i\\in S}\\alpha^{p}_{i}p_i+\\sum_{k\\in K}\\alpha^{f}_{k}f_k+\\sum_{v\\in V}\\alpha^{\\delta}_{v}\\delta_v-\\sum_{j\\in D}\\alpha^{d}_{j}d_j (2.29a) s.t. \\sum_{k\\in K^{rec}_n}f_k+\\sum_{i\\in S_n}p_i+\\sum_{v\\in V^{snd}_n}\\delta_v=\\sum_{k\\in K^{snd}_n}f_k+\\sum_{j\\in D_n}d_j+\\sum_{v\\in V^{rec}_n}\\delta_v, (\\pi_n) n\\in N (2.29b); f_{l^+}-f_{l^-}=B_l(\\theta_{snd(l)}-\\theta_{rec(l)}), l\\in L (2.29c); 0\\le\\sum_{j\\in D_n}d_j+\\sum_{v\\in V^{rec}_n}\\delta_v-\\sum_{v\\in V^{snd}_n}\\delta_v\\le\\bar d^{max}_n, (\\omega^{l}_n,\\omega^{u}_n) n\\in N (2.29d); d\\in C_d, p\\in C_p, \\theta\\in C_\\theta, \\delta\\in C_\\delta (2.29e).","paper":"42-data_center_space_time_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R014","quote":"The market clearing problem with spatial virtual links is: min_{d,p,f,\\theta,\\delta}\\sum_{i\\in S}\\alpha^{p}_{i}p_i+\\sum_{k\\in K}\\alpha^{f}_{k}f_k+\\sum_{v\\in V}\\alpha^{\\delta}_{v}\\delta_v-\\sum_{j\\in D}\\alpha^{d}_{j}d_j (2.29a) s.t. \\sum_{k\\in K^{rec}_n}f_k+\\sum_{i\\in S_n}p_i+\\sum_{v\\in V^{snd}_n}\\delta_v=\\sum_{k\\in K^{snd}_n}f_k+\\sum_{j\\in D_n}d_j+\\sum_{v\\in V^{rec}_n}\\delta_v, (\\pi_n) n\\in N (2.29b); f_{l^+}-f_{l^-}=B_l(\\theta_{snd(l)}-\\theta_{rec(l)}), l\\in L (2.29c); 0\\le\\sum_{j\\in D_n}d_j+\\sum_{v\\in V^{rec}_n}\\delta_v-\\sum_{v\\in V^{snd}_n}\\delta_v\\le\\bar d^{max}_n, (\\omega^{l}_n,\\omega^{u}_n) n\\in N (2.29d); d\\in C_d, p\\in C_p, \\theta\\in C_\\theta, \\delta\\in C_\\delta (2.29e)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e026","location":{"location_id":"src-1c03246cb9-p07-c2r3__v141-a-M04-bidding","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"2.5 Basic formulation with general virtual links (spatial)","anchor_text":"2.5. Basic formulation with general virtual links (spatial) We now generalize the concept of virtual","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"We let V be the set of all virtual links; each virtual link v\\in V has an associated bid price \\alpha^{\\delta}_{v}\\in R_+ and capacity \\bar\\delta_v\\in[0,\\infty). The cleared load shifts (virtual flows) are defined as \\delta_v\\in R_+ and are subject to capacity constraints \\delta_v\\in[0,\\bar\\delta_v]. We define V^{snd}_n:=\\{v\\in V | snd(v)=n\\}\\subseteq V, V^{rec}_n:=\\{v\\in V | rec(v)=n\\}\\subseteq V to be the set of sending and receiving virtual links at node n. Each flexible consumer j\\in D is associated with a set of virtual links V_j\\subseteq V. For each v\\in V_j, snd(v)=n_h(j) since each consumer can only bid flexibility going from its hub node to other alternative nodes.","paper":"42-data_center_space_time_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R014","quote":"We let V be the set of all virtual links; each virtual link v\\in V has an associated bid price \\alpha^{\\delta}_{v}\\in R_+ and capacity \\bar\\delta_v\\in[0,\\infty). The cleared load shifts (virtual flows) are defined as \\delta_v\\in R_+ and are subject to capacity constraints \\delta_v\\in[0,\\bar\\delta_v]. We define V^{snd}_n:=\\{v\\in V | snd(v)=n\\}\\subseteq V, V^{rec}_n:=\\{v\\in V | rec(v)=n\\}\\subseteq V to be the set of sending and receiving virtual links at node n. Each flexible consumer j\\in D is associated with a set of virtual links V_j\\subseteq V. For each v\\in V_j, snd(v)=n_h(j) since each consumer can only bid flexibility going from its hub node to other alternative nodes."}],"candidate_only":true,"classification_subtypes":["net_expenditure_accounting","reference_load_charge_and_shift_remuneration"],"projection_statuses":["clear"],"taxonomy":{"primary_family":"KG40-L8-SC-L8-e260aa47afe769d8","secondary_families":[],"stage":"L8","ontological_kind":"SC-L8-e260aa47afe769d8"},"semantic_definition":{"definition":"Account for participants' resource purchase costs and supply revenues; by source, retain the composition of production cost, transport cost, or demand value, forming a charge, a revenue, or a net amount, respectively.","identity_fields":["Economic purpose: account for the receipts and payments formed by resource transactions","Primary objects: purchase costs, supply revenues, and source-specified net amounts","Responsibility relation: the receipt-and-payment relation of a participant as resource buyer and as supplier"]},"source_units":[{"unit_id":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment","paper_id":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"settlement.payment","source_occurrences":[{"id":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R012::V::settlement.payment","rule_id":"v141-c-R012","market_meaning":"Microgrids pay for energy purchases and transportation of purchased energy, and receive payment for energy sold. Scope: Economic interpretation of energy transactions among interconnected islanded microgrids. Inter-microgrid energy transactions may incur a tax charged by the distribution network operator. Scope: Optional transaction-tax component of inter-microgrid energy-transfer costs. Condition: When the transfer-cost model includes a tax charged by the distribution network operator for energy transactions.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"settlement","context_text":"Economic interpretation of energy transactions among interconnected islanded microgrids. | Optional transaction-tax component of inter-microgrid energy-transfer costs.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g07-e007","v141-b-g23-e023"]},"constraint_refs":["27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R012::v141-c-R012-C001","27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R012::v141-c-R012-C002","27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R012::v141-c-R012-C003"],"decision_anchor":"settlement.payment","source_rule_sha256":"b3c542594673edd16b09ca916a57fa47c3a9e87bf58c053c8eb28533aa42c605"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R012-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Microgrids pay for energy purchases and transportation of purchased energy, and receive payment for energy sold. Scope: Economic interpretation of energy transactions among interconnected islanded microgrids.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["execution.energy_trade","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g23-e023"],"ref":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R012::v141-c-R012-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R012","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g23-e023","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III-A. Decentralizing the Problem","anchor_text":", g ining interpretation: Each Lagrange multiplier λi may be under- costood as the price per energy ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Each Lagrange multiplier λ_i may be understood as the price per energy unit requested by μG-i to sell energy to its neighbors. Then, Lagrangian function (7) can be seen as the “net expenditure” (the opposite of the net income) for μG-i, i.e., each μG pays for producing energy, for buying energy, and for transporting the energy it buys. Conversely, the μG is paid for the energy it sells. By solving problem (6), μG-i is thus maximizing its benefit for some given selling (λ_i[k]) and buying (λ_j[k], j≠i) prices per energy unit."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R012-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Inter-microgrid energy transactions may incur a tax charged by the distribution network operator. Scope: Optional transaction-tax component of inter-microgrid energy-transfer costs. Condition: When the transfer-cost model includes a tax charged by the distribution network operator for energy transactions.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["execution.energy_trade","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g07-e007"],"ref":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R012::v141-c-R012-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R012","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g07-e007","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p02-c2r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. System Model","anchor_text":"0 MWh for all i, j = 1, . . . , M. ach Next, let Ci(E(g)i ) and γ(Ei,j) be the costs of producing E(","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Next, let C_i(E_i^{(g)}) and γ(E_{i,j}) be the costs of producing E_i^{(g)} units of energy at μG-i and transferring E_{i,j} units of energy on the μG-i−μG-j link, respectively. This transferring cost function may model several factors. For example, the distribution network operator, as the enabler of the energy transfer between the μGs, may charge a tax for energy transactions. In addition, this transfer cost function may also account for line congestions by introducing soft constraints on the maximum capacity of the line (see Section II-A for further information). Although the solution in the following may be extended to the case where different links have different transfer cost functions, we assume that γ(·) is common among all the links in order to avoid further complexity. As it will be clearer later, our approach is quite general and only requires that all cost functions (both production and transport) satisfy some mild convexity constraints, which are summarized in Section II-A."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R012-C003","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"At the equilibrium price vector, μG-i’s net expenditure (payment outflow minus income) equals the generation cost of its net energy output plus the transfer costs and purchase payments for energy bought from the suppliers in S_*, minus the revenue from the energy it sells, as given by the source’s Case 6 net-expenditure formula.","expression":"C^{(l)}_i(\\lambda^*) = C_i\\left(E^{(c)}_i + \\varepsilon^{(s)}_i - \\sum_{j\\in S_*} E_{j,i}\\right) + \\sum_{j\\in S_*} \\gamma(E_{j,i}) + \\sum_{j\\in S_*} \\lambda^*_j E_{j,i} - \\lambda^*_i \\varepsilon^{(s)}_i","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["execution.energy_trade","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R012-x-Eji","v141-c-R012-x-eps"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R012-theta-Ci","v141-c-R012-theta-gamma","v141-c-R012-theta-Ec","v141-c-R012-theta-lambda-i","v141-c-R012-theta-lambda-j"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e022","v141-b-g23-e023"],"ref":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R012::v141-c-R012-C003","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R012","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e022","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p06-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"III-C. Interpretation and Summary","anchor_text":"diture Ci (λ ) in (7), where λ stands for the optimal point of (5). For the sake of brevity, we prov","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"In Case 6, the net expenditure reads\nC^{(l)}_i(\\lambda^*) = C_i\\left(E^{(c)}_i + \\varepsilon^{(s)}_i - \\sum_{j \\in S_*} E_{j,i}\\right) + \\sum_{j \\in S_*} \\gamma(E_{j,i}) + \\sum_{j \\in S_*} \\lambda^*_j E_{j,i} - \\lambda^*_i \\varepsilon^{(s)}_i\nwhere (\\varepsilon^{(s)}_i, \\{E_{j,i}\\}) = (\\varepsilon^{(s)}_i(\\lambda^*), \\{E_{j,i}(\\lambda^*)\\}) is now the min-imum point of (6) for \\lambda = \\lambda^*."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g23-e023","location":{"location_id":"src-f5dd785b87-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III-A. Decentralizing the Problem","anchor_text":", g ining interpretation: Each Lagrange multiplier λi may be under- costood as the price per energy ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Each Lagrange multiplier λ_i may be understood as the price per energy unit requested by μG-i to sell energy to its neighbors. Then, Lagrangian function (7) can be seen as the “net expenditure” (the opposite of the net income) for μG-i, i.e., each μG pays for producing energy, for buying energy, and for transporting the energy it buys. Conversely, the μG is paid for the energy it sells. By solving problem (6), μG-i is thus maximizing its benefit for some given selling (λ_i[k]) and buying (λ_j[k], j≠i) prices per energy unit."}]}],"variables":[{"variable_id":"v141-c-R012-x-Eji","symbol":"E_{j,i}","meaning":"Energy quantity bought by μG-i from μG-j in the local subproblem, used here as the settled purchase quantity in μG-i’s net-expenditure accounting; in Case 6 it is positive for j in S_* and zero otherwise.","kind":"operational","domain":"nonnegative real energy quantity, E_{j,i} ≥ 0 (MWh)","unit":"MWh","indices":["j","i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g06-e006","v141-b-g15-e015","v141-b-g02-e022"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R012","declaration_ref":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R012::x::v141-c-R012-x-Eji"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R012-x-eps","symbol":"\\varepsilon^{(s)}_i","meaning":"Energy sold by μG-i in the local subproblem, used here as the sold-energy quantity whose revenue enters μG-i’s net-expenditure accounting.","kind":"operational","domain":"nonnegative real energy quantity, \\varepsilon^{(s)}_i ≥ 0 (MWh)","unit":"MWh","indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g15-e015","v141-b-g02-e022","v141-b-g23-e023"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R012","declaration_ref":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R012::x::v141-c-R012-x-eps"}],"parameters":[{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R012-theta-Ci","symbol":"C_i(\\cdot)","meaning":"Generation cost function of μG-i, mapping generated energy to cost; it appears as the production-cost term in net expenditure.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"symbolic cost function C_i(\\cdot); no single numeric value is fixed for this rule"},"unit":"USD","indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g07-e007","v141-b-g02-e022"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R012","declaration_ref":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R012::theta::v141-c-R012-theta-Ci"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R012-theta-gamma","symbol":"\\gamma(\\cdot)","meaning":"Common transfer cost function for energy transferred on a μG link; the source states it may model a distribution-network-operator tax and congestion soft constraints.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"symbolic transfer-cost function \\gamma(\\cdot); no single numeric value is fixed for this rule"},"unit":"USD","indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g07-e007","v141-b-g02-e022"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R012","declaration_ref":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R012::theta::v141-c-R012-theta-gamma"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R012-theta-Ec","symbol":"E^{(c)}_i","meaning":"Local energy consumption/load of μG-i, treated as a fixed input in the payment accounting; the source case analysis assumes positive load and also discusses a zero-load extension.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"symbolic local consumption/load parameter E^{(c)}_i"},"unit":"MWh","indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g04-e004","v141-b-g02-e022","v141-b-g37-e037"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R012","declaration_ref":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R012::theta::v141-c-R012-theta-Ec"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R012-theta-lambda-i","symbol":"\\lambda^*_i","meaning":"Equilibrium per-unit selling price requested by μG-i, interpreted as the price per energy unit for energy sold and used to value μG-i’s sales revenue in the settlement accounting.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"equilibrium price \\lambda^*_i; no numeric value is fixed in this rule"},"unit":"USD/MWh","indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e019","v141-b-g02-e023","v141-b-g23-e023"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R012","declaration_ref":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R012::theta::v141-c-R012-theta-lambda-i"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R012-theta-lambda-j","symbol":"\\lambda^*_j","meaning":"Equilibrium per-unit buying price paid by μG-i to μG-j for purchased energy j→i in the settlement accounting.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"equilibrium price \\lambda^*_j; no numeric value is fixed in this rule"},"unit":"USD/MWh","indices":["j"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e019","v141-b-g02-e022","v141-b-g02-e023"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R012","declaration_ref":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R012::theta::v141-c-R012-theta-lambda-j"}]},{"unit_id":"42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-a-M03::settlement.payment","paper_id":"42-data_center_space_time_flexibility","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M03","function":"settlement.payment","source_occurrences":[{"id":"42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-c-R012::V::settlement.payment","rule_id":"v141-c-R012","market_meaning":"The consumer's load charge uses the hub price, while its virtual-link service is remunerated by the hub-minus-alternative-node price difference multiplied by shifted load. Scope: Hub-based virtual-link market for a flexible consumer offering alternative delivery nodes.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"settlement","context_text":"Hub-based virtual-link market for a flexible consumer offering alternative delivery nodes.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e024"]},"constraint_refs":["42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-c-R012::v141-c-R012-C001","42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-c-R012::v141-c-R012-C002"],"decision_anchor":"settlement.payment","source_rule_sha256":"54fa3cfd25ff88537520b3c5b754f1d4f3c6a3c7e60306a9b5451e93a0071809"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R012-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The consumer's load charge uses the hub price, while its virtual-link service is remunerated by the hub-minus-alternative-node price difference multiplied by shifted load. Scope: Hub-based virtual-link market for a flexible consumer offering alternative delivery nodes.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e024"],"ref":"42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-c-R012::v141-c-R012-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R012","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e024","location":{"location_id":"src-1c03246cb9-p07-c1r4__v141-a-M03-pricing","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"2.4 Basic formulation with virtual links","anchor_text":"∑ 𝑑− 𝛿𝑛ℎ,𝑛≥0 (2.25d) a 𝑛∈𝑑 ce 𝑓𝑙+ −𝑓𝑙−= 𝐵𝑙(𝜃snd(𝑙) −𝜃rec(𝑙)), 𝑙∈ (2.25e) pr to 𝑝∈𝑝, 𝜃∈𝜃 (2.25f) ","equation_id":"2.26","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The Lagrange function (2.24) reveals that virtual links are a service offered by the consumer. The market remunerates the consumer for the provision of this service via the profit \\sum_{n\\in N_d}(\\pi_{n_h}-\\pi_n)\\delta_{n_h,n}. This highlights that load-shifting is incentivized whenever there is a nodal price \\pi_n that is lower than the price at the hub node \\pi_{n_h}. This is analogous to how transmission is remunerated (based on nodal price differences). The market also charges the consumer via the profit (\\alpha^d-\\pi_{n_h})d; consequently, the flexible consumer acts as a prosumer and has total profit: (\\alpha^d-\\pi_{n_h})d+\\sum_{n\\in N_d}(\\pi_{n_h}-\\pi_n)\\delta_{n_h,n}=(\\alpha^d-\\pi_{n_h})\\left(d-\\sum_{n\\in N_d}\\delta_{n_h,n}\\right)+\\sum_{n\\in N_d}(\\alpha^d-\\pi_n)\\delta_{n_h,n} (2.26)."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R012-C002","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The flexible consumer's total profit in the hub-based virtual-link market equals the hub-load term (α^d − π_{n_h})d plus the virtual-link remuneration Σ_{n∈N_d}(π_{n_h} − π_n)δ_{n_h,n}, and the source states the equivalent regrouping (α^d − π_{n_h})(d − Σ_{n∈N_d}δ_{n_h,n}) + Σ_{n∈N_d}(α^d − π_n)δ_{n_h,n}.","expression":"(\\alpha^d-\\pi_{n_h})d+\\sum_{n\\in N_d}(\\pi_{n_h}-\\pi_n)\\delta_{n_h,n}=(\\alpha^d-\\pi_{n_h})\\left(d-\\sum_{n\\in N_d}\\delta_{n_h,n}\\right)+\\sum_{n\\in N_d}(\\alpha^d-\\pi_n)\\delta_{n_h,n}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R012-x-d","v141-c-R012-x-delta"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R012-theta-alpha-d","v141-c-R012-theta-pi"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e024"],"ref":"42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-c-R012::v141-c-R012-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R012","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e024","location":{"location_id":"src-1c03246cb9-p07-c1r4__v141-a-M03-pricing","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"2.4 Basic formulation with virtual links","anchor_text":"∑ 𝑑− 𝛿𝑛ℎ,𝑛≥0 (2.25d) a 𝑛∈𝑑 ce 𝑓𝑙+ −𝑓𝑙−= 𝐵𝑙(𝜃snd(𝑙) −𝜃rec(𝑙)), 𝑙∈ (2.25e) pr to 𝑝∈𝑝, 𝜃∈𝜃 (2.25f) ","equation_id":"2.26","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The Lagrange function (2.24) reveals that virtual links are a service offered by the consumer. The market remunerates the consumer for the provision of this service via the profit \\sum_{n\\in N_d}(\\pi_{n_h}-\\pi_n)\\delta_{n_h,n}. This highlights that load-shifting is incentivized whenever there is a nodal price \\pi_n that is lower than the price at the hub node \\pi_{n_h}. This is analogous to how transmission is remunerated (based on nodal price differences). The market also charges the consumer via the profit (\\alpha^d-\\pi_{n_h})d; consequently, the flexible consumer acts as a prosumer and has total profit: (\\alpha^d-\\pi_{n_h})d+\\sum_{n\\in N_d}(\\pi_{n_h}-\\pi_n)\\delta_{n_h,n}=(\\alpha^d-\\pi_{n_h})\\left(d-\\sum_{n\\in N_d}\\delta_{n_h,n}\\right)+\\sum_{n\\in N_d}(\\alpha^d-\\pi_n)\\delta_{n_h,n} (2.26)."}]}],"variables":[{"variable_id":"v141-c-R012-x-d","symbol":"d","meaning":"Cleared load requested by the flexible consumer at its hub node in the hub-based virtual-link formulation.","kind":"operational","domain":"0 \\le d \\le \\bar d","unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e020","v141-b-g02-e024"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R012","declaration_ref":"42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-c-R012::x::v141-c-R012-x-d"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R012-x-delta","symbol":"\\delta_{n_h,n}","meaning":"Cleared amount of load shifted from hub node n_h to alternate delivery node n via a virtual link.","kind":"operational","domain":"\\delta_{n_h,n} \\ge 0","unit":null,"indices":["n_h (hub node)","n \\in N_d"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e019","v141-b-g02-e020","v141-b-g02-e024"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R012","declaration_ref":"42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-c-R012::x::v141-c-R012-x-delta"}],"parameters":[{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R012-theta-alpha-d","symbol":"\\alpha^d","meaning":"Bid price submitted by the flexible consumer for requested load at its hub node.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e019","v141-b-g02-e024"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R012","declaration_ref":"42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-c-R012::theta::v141-c-R012-theta-alpha-d"},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R012-theta-pi","symbol":"\\pi_n","meaning":"Cleared nodal price (LMP) at node n; the settlement expression uses the hub-node value \\pi_{n_h} and alternate-node values \\pi_n.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":["n \\in N"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e020","v141-b-g02-e024"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R012","declaration_ref":"42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-c-R012::theta::v141-c-R012-theta-pi"}]},{"unit_id":"42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-a-M04::settlement.payment","paper_id":"42-data_center_space_time_flexibility","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M04","function":"settlement.payment","source_occurrences":[{"id":"42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-c-R014::V::settlement.payment","rule_id":"v141-c-R014","market_meaning":"Each consumer is charged the electricity price at its hub node and remunerated for shifting services; virtual links are paid using the price difference between sending and receiving nodes. Scope: General spatial virtual-link market; consumers and their virtual-link shifting services.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"settlement","context_text":"General spatial virtual-link market; consumers and their virtual-link shifting services.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e031"]},"constraint_refs":["42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-c-R014::v141-c-R014-C001","42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-c-R014::v141-c-R014-C002","42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-c-R014::v141-c-R014-C003","42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-c-R014::v141-c-R014-C004"],"decision_anchor":"settlement.payment","source_rule_sha256":"54e8851d9b2167717950318c62233deb81e9104139ab7ff1e7e83a5b24c64f39"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R014-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Each consumer is charged the electricity price at its hub node and remunerated for shifting services; virtual links are paid using the price difference between sending and receiving nodes. Scope: General spatial virtual-link market; consumers and their virtual-link shifting services.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e031"],"ref":"42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-c-R014::v141-c-R014-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R014","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e031","location":{"location_id":"src-1c03246cb9-p08-c1r3__v141-a-M04-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"2.5 Basic formulation with general virtual links (spatial)","anchor_text":"(e.g., data transfer costs). In this market, each consumer 𝑗∈is charged with the electricity price ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"where the set C_{\\delta}:=\\{\\delta | \\delta_v\\in[0,\\bar\\delta_v] \\forall v\\in V\\} captures the capacity constraints for virtual links. Comparing this formulation with the previous formulation (2.23), we observe that the social surplus (2.29a) now captures the operational cost of virtual links; moreover, the balance constraints (2.29b) now include spatial virtual shifts. The dual variables associated with the computing capacity constraints (2.29d) are denoted \\omega^{l}_n and \\omega^{u}_n, respectively. Note that the operational cost of virtual links resembles that of operational costs of physical transmission and, as the name suggest, these capture costs associated with load shifting (e.g., data transfer costs). In this market, each consumer j\\in D is charged with the electricity price at the hub node and also remunerated by the shifting service provided through virtual links. As shown in Section 2.4, the market pays off virtual links by the price difference between the sending and receiving nodes."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R014-C002","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"For each consumer j in D, the consumer is charged the electricity price at its hub node for its cleared load d_j, so the consumer charge is charge_j = pi_{n_h(j)} d_j.","expression":"\\text{charge}_j = \\pi_{n_h(j)} d_j,\\quad j\\in D","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R014-X001"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R014-T001"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e031","v141-b-g02-e030","v141-b-g02-e026"],"ref":"42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-c-R014::v141-c-R014-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R014","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e031","location":{"location_id":"src-1c03246cb9-p08-c1r3__v141-a-M04-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"2.5 Basic formulation with general virtual links (spatial)","anchor_text":"(e.g., data transfer costs). In this market, each consumer 𝑗∈is charged with the electricity price ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"where the set C_{\\delta}:=\\{\\delta | \\delta_v\\in[0,\\bar\\delta_v] \\forall v\\in V\\} captures the capacity constraints for virtual links. Comparing this formulation with the previous formulation (2.23), we observe that the social surplus (2.29a) now captures the operational cost of virtual links; moreover, the balance constraints (2.29b) now include spatial virtual shifts. The dual variables associated with the computing capacity constraints (2.29d) are denoted \\omega^{l}_n and \\omega^{u}_n, respectively. Note that the operational cost of virtual links resembles that of operational costs of physical transmission and, as the name suggest, these capture costs associated with load shifting (e.g., data transfer costs). In this market, each consumer j\\in D is charged with the electricity price at the hub node and also remunerated by the shifting service provided through virtual links. As shown in Section 2.4, the market pays off virtual links by the price difference between the sending and receiving nodes."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e030","location":{"location_id":"src-1c03246cb9-p08-c1r2__v141-a-M04-clearing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"2.5 Basic formulation with general virtual links (spatial)","anchor_text":"∑ ∑ + 𝑑𝑗+ 𝛿𝑣, (𝜋𝑛) 𝑛∈ (2.29b) m 𝑗∈𝑛 𝑣∈rec𝑛 𝑑, 𝑓𝑙+ −𝑓𝑙−= 𝐵𝑙(𝜃snd(𝑙) −𝜃rec(𝑙)), 𝑙∈ (2.29c) s. ∑ ∑ ","equation_id":"2.29","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The market clearing problem with spatial virtual links is: min_{d,p,f,\\theta,\\delta}\\sum_{i\\in S}\\alpha^{p}_{i}p_i+\\sum_{k\\in K}\\alpha^{f}_{k}f_k+\\sum_{v\\in V}\\alpha^{\\delta}_{v}\\delta_v-\\sum_{j\\in D}\\alpha^{d}_{j}d_j (2.29a) s.t. \\sum_{k\\in K^{rec}_n}f_k+\\sum_{i\\in S_n}p_i+\\sum_{v\\in V^{snd}_n}\\delta_v=\\sum_{k\\in K^{snd}_n}f_k+\\sum_{j\\in D_n}d_j+\\sum_{v\\in V^{rec}_n}\\delta_v, (\\pi_n) n\\in N (2.29b); f_{l^+}-f_{l^-}=B_l(\\theta_{snd(l)}-\\theta_{rec(l)}), l\\in L (2.29c); 0\\le\\sum_{j\\in D_n}d_j+\\sum_{v\\in V^{rec}_n}\\delta_v-\\sum_{v\\in V^{snd}_n}\\delta_v\\le\\bar d^{max}_n, (\\omega^{l}_n,\\omega^{u}_n) n\\in N (2.29d); d\\in C_d, p\\in C_p, \\theta\\in C_\\theta, \\delta\\in C_\\delta (2.29e)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e026","location":{"location_id":"src-1c03246cb9-p07-c2r3__v141-a-M04-bidding","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"2.5 Basic formulation with general virtual links (spatial)","anchor_text":"2.5. Basic formulation with general virtual links (spatial) We now generalize the concept of virtual","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"We let V be the set of all virtual links; each virtual link v\\in V has an associated bid price \\alpha^{\\delta}_{v}\\in R_+ and capacity \\bar\\delta_v\\in[0,\\infty). The cleared load shifts (virtual flows) are defined as \\delta_v\\in R_+ and are subject to capacity constraints \\delta_v\\in[0,\\bar\\delta_v]. We define V^{snd}_n:=\\{v\\in V | snd(v)=n\\}\\subseteq V, V^{rec}_n:=\\{v\\in V | rec(v)=n\\}\\subseteq V to be the set of sending and receiving virtual links at node n. Each flexible consumer j\\in D is associated with a set of virtual links V_j\\subseteq V. For each v\\in V_j, snd(v)=n_h(j) since each consumer can only bid flexibility going from its hub node to other alternative nodes."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R014-C003","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"For each virtual link v in V, the market pays the virtual link by the price difference between its sending and receiving nodes, so the virtual-link payment is payment_v = (pi_{snd(v)} - pi_{rec(v)}) delta_v.","expression":"\\text{payment}_v = (\\pi_{snd(v)} - \\pi_{rec(v)})\\,\\delta_v,\\quad v\\in V","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R014-X002"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R014-T001"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e031","v141-b-g02-e026","v141-b-g02-e030"],"ref":"42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-c-R014::v141-c-R014-C003","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R014","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e031","location":{"location_id":"src-1c03246cb9-p08-c1r3__v141-a-M04-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"2.5 Basic formulation with general virtual links (spatial)","anchor_text":"(e.g., data transfer costs). In this market, each consumer 𝑗∈is charged with the electricity price ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"where the set C_{\\delta}:=\\{\\delta | \\delta_v\\in[0,\\bar\\delta_v] \\forall v\\in V\\} captures the capacity constraints for virtual links. Comparing this formulation with the previous formulation (2.23), we observe that the social surplus (2.29a) now captures the operational cost of virtual links; moreover, the balance constraints (2.29b) now include spatial virtual shifts. The dual variables associated with the computing capacity constraints (2.29d) are denoted \\omega^{l}_n and \\omega^{u}_n, respectively. Note that the operational cost of virtual links resembles that of operational costs of physical transmission and, as the name suggest, these capture costs associated with load shifting (e.g., data transfer costs). In this market, each consumer j\\in D is charged with the electricity price at the hub node and also remunerated by the shifting service provided through virtual links. As shown in Section 2.4, the market pays off virtual links by the price difference between the sending and receiving nodes."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e026","location":{"location_id":"src-1c03246cb9-p07-c2r3__v141-a-M04-bidding","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"2.5 Basic formulation with general virtual links (spatial)","anchor_text":"2.5. Basic formulation with general virtual links (spatial) We now generalize the concept of virtual","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"We let V be the set of all virtual links; each virtual link v\\in V has an associated bid price \\alpha^{\\delta}_{v}\\in R_+ and capacity \\bar\\delta_v\\in[0,\\infty). The cleared load shifts (virtual flows) are defined as \\delta_v\\in R_+ and are subject to capacity constraints \\delta_v\\in[0,\\bar\\delta_v]. We define V^{snd}_n:=\\{v\\in V | snd(v)=n\\}\\subseteq V, V^{rec}_n:=\\{v\\in V | rec(v)=n\\}\\subseteq V to be the set of sending and receiving virtual links at node n. Each flexible consumer j\\in D is associated with a set of virtual links V_j\\subseteq V. For each v\\in V_j, snd(v)=n_h(j) since each consumer can only bid flexibility going from its hub node to other alternative nodes."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e030","location":{"location_id":"src-1c03246cb9-p08-c1r2__v141-a-M04-clearing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"2.5 Basic formulation with general virtual links (spatial)","anchor_text":"∑ ∑ + 𝑑𝑗+ 𝛿𝑣, (𝜋𝑛) 𝑛∈ (2.29b) m 𝑗∈𝑛 𝑣∈rec𝑛 𝑑, 𝑓𝑙+ −𝑓𝑙−= 𝐵𝑙(𝜃snd(𝑙) −𝜃rec(𝑙)), 𝑙∈ (2.29c) s. ∑ ∑ ","equation_id":"2.29","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The market clearing problem with spatial virtual links is: min_{d,p,f,\\theta,\\delta}\\sum_{i\\in S}\\alpha^{p}_{i}p_i+\\sum_{k\\in K}\\alpha^{f}_{k}f_k+\\sum_{v\\in V}\\alpha^{\\delta}_{v}\\delta_v-\\sum_{j\\in D}\\alpha^{d}_{j}d_j (2.29a) s.t. \\sum_{k\\in K^{rec}_n}f_k+\\sum_{i\\in S_n}p_i+\\sum_{v\\in V^{snd}_n}\\delta_v=\\sum_{k\\in K^{snd}_n}f_k+\\sum_{j\\in D_n}d_j+\\sum_{v\\in V^{rec}_n}\\delta_v, (\\pi_n) n\\in N (2.29b); f_{l^+}-f_{l^-}=B_l(\\theta_{snd(l)}-\\theta_{rec(l)}), l\\in L (2.29c); 0\\le\\sum_{j\\in D_n}d_j+\\sum_{v\\in V^{rec}_n}\\delta_v-\\sum_{v\\in V^{snd}_n}\\delta_v\\le\\bar d^{max}_n, (\\omega^{l}_n,\\omega^{u}_n) n\\in N (2.29d); d\\in C_d, p\\in C_p, \\theta\\in C_\\theta, \\delta\\in C_\\delta (2.29e)."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R014-C004","kind":"mathematical","role":"supporting","statement":"For each consumer j in D, the shifting-service remuneration received through the consumer's virtual links V_j is remuneration_j = sum over v in V_j of (pi_{snd(v)} - pi_{rec(v)}) delta_v.","expression":"\\text{remuneration}_j = \\sum_{v\\in V_j} (\\pi_{snd(v)} - \\pi_{rec(v)})\\,\\delta_v,\\quad j\\in D","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R014-X002"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R014-T001"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e031","v141-b-g02-e026","v141-b-g02-e030"],"ref":"42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-c-R014::v141-c-R014-C004","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R014","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e031","location":{"location_id":"src-1c03246cb9-p08-c1r3__v141-a-M04-pricing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"2.5 Basic formulation with general virtual links (spatial)","anchor_text":"(e.g., data transfer costs). In this market, each consumer 𝑗∈is charged with the electricity price ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"where the set C_{\\delta}:=\\{\\delta | \\delta_v\\in[0,\\bar\\delta_v] \\forall v\\in V\\} captures the capacity constraints for virtual links. Comparing this formulation with the previous formulation (2.23), we observe that the social surplus (2.29a) now captures the operational cost of virtual links; moreover, the balance constraints (2.29b) now include spatial virtual shifts. The dual variables associated with the computing capacity constraints (2.29d) are denoted \\omega^{l}_n and \\omega^{u}_n, respectively. Note that the operational cost of virtual links resembles that of operational costs of physical transmission and, as the name suggest, these capture costs associated with load shifting (e.g., data transfer costs). In this market, each consumer j\\in D is charged with the electricity price at the hub node and also remunerated by the shifting service provided through virtual links. As shown in Section 2.4, the market pays off virtual links by the price difference between the sending and receiving nodes."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e026","location":{"location_id":"src-1c03246cb9-p07-c2r3__v141-a-M04-bidding","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"2.5 Basic formulation with general virtual links (spatial)","anchor_text":"2.5. Basic formulation with general virtual links (spatial) We now generalize the concept of virtual","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"We let V be the set of all virtual links; each virtual link v\\in V has an associated bid price \\alpha^{\\delta}_{v}\\in R_+ and capacity \\bar\\delta_v\\in[0,\\infty). The cleared load shifts (virtual flows) are defined as \\delta_v\\in R_+ and are subject to capacity constraints \\delta_v\\in[0,\\bar\\delta_v]. We define V^{snd}_n:=\\{v\\in V | snd(v)=n\\}\\subseteq V, V^{rec}_n:=\\{v\\in V | rec(v)=n\\}\\subseteq V to be the set of sending and receiving virtual links at node n. Each flexible consumer j\\in D is associated with a set of virtual links V_j\\subseteq V. For each v\\in V_j, snd(v)=n_h(j) since each consumer can only bid flexibility going from its hub node to other alternative nodes."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e030","location":{"location_id":"src-1c03246cb9-p08-c1r2__v141-a-M04-clearing","page":8,"printed_page":null,"section":"2.5 Basic formulation with general virtual links (spatial)","anchor_text":"∑ ∑ + 𝑑𝑗+ 𝛿𝑣, (𝜋𝑛) 𝑛∈ (2.29b) m 𝑗∈𝑛 𝑣∈rec𝑛 𝑑, 𝑓𝑙+ −𝑓𝑙−= 𝐵𝑙(𝜃snd(𝑙) −𝜃rec(𝑙)), 𝑙∈ (2.29c) s. ∑ ∑ ","equation_id":"2.29","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The market clearing problem with spatial virtual links is: min_{d,p,f,\\theta,\\delta}\\sum_{i\\in S}\\alpha^{p}_{i}p_i+\\sum_{k\\in K}\\alpha^{f}_{k}f_k+\\sum_{v\\in V}\\alpha^{\\delta}_{v}\\delta_v-\\sum_{j\\in D}\\alpha^{d}_{j}d_j (2.29a) s.t. \\sum_{k\\in K^{rec}_n}f_k+\\sum_{i\\in S_n}p_i+\\sum_{v\\in V^{snd}_n}\\delta_v=\\sum_{k\\in K^{snd}_n}f_k+\\sum_{j\\in D_n}d_j+\\sum_{v\\in V^{rec}_n}\\delta_v, (\\pi_n) n\\in N (2.29b); f_{l^+}-f_{l^-}=B_l(\\theta_{snd(l)}-\\theta_{rec(l)}), l\\in L (2.29c); 0\\le\\sum_{j\\in D_n}d_j+\\sum_{v\\in V^{rec}_n}\\delta_v-\\sum_{v\\in V^{snd}_n}\\delta_v\\le\\bar d^{max}_n, (\\omega^{l}_n,\\omega^{u}_n) n\\in N (2.29d); d\\in C_d, p\\in C_p, \\theta\\in C_\\theta, \\delta\\in C_\\delta (2.29e)."}]}],"variables":[{"variable_id":"v141-c-R014-X001","symbol":"d_j","meaning":"Cleared load allocation (load withdrawn) for consumer j, used as a settlement input.","kind":"operational","domain":"d_j \\in C_d (cleared-load domain in the spatial clearing formulation (2.29e))","unit":null,"indices":["j\\in D"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e030","v141-b-g02-e031"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R014","declaration_ref":"42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-c-R014::x::v141-c-R014-X001"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R014-X002","symbol":"\\delta_v","meaning":"Cleared load shift (virtual flow) on virtual link v, used as a settlement input.","kind":"operational","domain":"[0,\\bar\\delta_v]","unit":null,"indices":["v\\in V"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e026","v141-b-g02-e031"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R014","declaration_ref":"42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-c-R014::x::v141-c-R014-X002"}],"parameters":[{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R014-T001","symbol":"\\pi_n","meaning":"Cleared nodal electricity price at node n (dual of nodal balance), used to charge consumers and remunerate virtual links.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"cleared nodal price \\pi_n"},"unit":null,"indices":["n\\in N"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e030","v141-b-g02-e031"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R014","declaration_ref":"42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-c-R014::theta::v141-c-R014-T001"}]}],"source_definitions":[{"unit_id":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment","stage":"L8","subtype":"net_expenditure_accounting","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"Account for the microgrid's generation cost, electricity-purchase payments, and the transport cost of purchased energy, deducting electricity-sale revenue. The Case 6 equilibrium point is C_i^{(l)}(λ^*)=C_i(E_i^{(c)}+ε_i^{(s)}−∑_{j∈S_*}E_{j,i})+∑_{j∈S_*}γ(E_{j,i})+∑_{j∈S_*}λ_j^*E_{j,i}−λ_i^*ε_i^{(s)}.","form_kind":"mathematical","identity_fields":["Object: monetary costs and revenues of the participant's transactions and production","Core relation: generation cost plus transport cost and electricity-purchase payments, minus electricity-sale revenue","Output: the participant's net expenditure"],"variation_fields":["The listed formula is restricted to Case 6, the equilibrium price λ^*, and the corresponding locally optimal quantities","Electricity purchases are accounted as payable at the supplier price λ_j^*; electricity-sale revenue is calculated at the participant's own price λ_i^*","γ may include the transaction tax levied by the distribution-network operator; it is an optional cost component and is not charged again separately","In the source model, γ is the transport-cost function shared by each connection","Generation cost is an economic-accounting item and is not specified as a payment to a counterparty"],"constraint_refs":["27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R012::v141-c-R012-C001","27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R012::v141-c-R012-C002","27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R012::v141-c-R012-C003"],"paper_id":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"settlement.payment","template_code":"JOC"},{"unit_id":"42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-a-M03::settlement.payment","stage":"L8","subtype":"reference_load_charge_and_shift_remuneration","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"The consumer pays π_{n_h}d for the total cleared requested quantity and receives migration-service remuneration of Σ_{n∈N_d}(π_{n_h}−π_n)δ_{n_h,n}; demand value minus the electricity charge plus this remuneration constitutes the consumer's total profit.","form_kind":"mathematical","identity_fields":["Object: the consumer's electricity purchase and migration service","Operation: charge at the reference-node price, and pay migration remuneration according to the send-minus-receive price difference","Output: load charge, service revenue, and the consumer's net benefit"],"variation_fields":["The reference node is the hub n_h","The consumer also provides virtual-link service","The remuneration uses the signed price difference","The demand value in profit is α^d d"],"constraint_refs":["42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-c-R012::v141-c-R012-C001","42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-c-R012::v141-c-R012-C002"],"paper_id":"42-data_center_space_time_flexibility","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M03","function":"settlement.payment","template_code":"JOC"},{"unit_id":"42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-a-M04::settlement.payment","stage":"L8","subtype":"reference_load_charge_and_shift_remuneration","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"Consumer j pays π_{n_h(j)}d_j; each virtual link receives (π_snd(v)−π_rec(v))δ_v, and the consumer's migration revenue is aggregated over that consumer's virtual-link set V_j.","form_kind":"mathematical","identity_fields":["Object: consumer load and its spatial migration service","Operation: hub price times load quantity; send-minus-receive price difference times migration quantity","Output: the consumer's electricity charge and the service revenue aggregated by virtual-link ownership"],"variation_fields":["Each consumer's hub n_h(j)","Virtual-link ownership set V_j","Unadjusted nodal prices π_n are used","The remuneration retains the sign of the price difference"],"constraint_refs":["42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-c-R014::v141-c-R014-C002","42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-c-R014::v141-c-R014-C003","42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-c-R014::v141-c-R014-C004"],"paper_id":"42-data_center_space_time_flexibility","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M04","function":"settlement.payment","template_code":"JOC"}],"source_variants":[{"unit_id":"27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment","retained_variants":["Case 6 equilibrium point: C_i^{(l)}(λ^*)=C_i(E_i^{(c)}+ε_i^{(s)}−∑_{j∈S_*}E_{j,i})+∑_{j∈S_*}γ(E_{j,i})+∑_{j∈S_*}λ_j^*E_{j,i}−λ_i^*ε_i^{(s)}, using the corresponding locally optimal quantities.","Electricity purchases are paid at the supplier price λ_j^*; electricity sales are reckoned as revenue at the participant's own price λ_i^*.","γ is the transport-cost function shared by each connection and may include a transaction tax of the distribution-network operator; that tax is not charged again separately.","Generation cost is an economic-accounting item and is not specified as a payment to a counterparty."],"evidence_refs":["27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment::projection"]},{"unit_id":"42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-a-M03::settlement.payment","retained_variants":["The electricity charge for the total cleared requested quantity is π_{n_h}d; the reference node is the hub n_h.","The consumer concurrently serves as the virtual-link service provider; the migration remuneration is ∑_{n∈N_d}(π_{n_h}−π_n)δ_{n_h,n}, and the signed price difference is retained.","Total profit is the demand value α^d d minus the electricity charge plus the migration remuneration; demand value is not treated as a monetary receipt."],"evidence_refs":["42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-a-M03::settlement.payment::projection"]},{"unit_id":"42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-a-M04::settlement.payment","retained_variants":["The electricity charge of consumer j is π_{n_h(j)}d_j, using that consumer's own hub n_h(j).","The revenue of each virtual link is (π_snd(v)−π_rec(v))δ_v, aggregated over the V_j owned by the consumer.","Unadjusted nodal prices π_n are used, and the sign of the send-minus-receive price difference is retained."],"evidence_refs":["42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-a-M04::settlement.payment::projection"]}],"functional":{"is_child":false,"subdivision_pending":false,"baseline_parent_id":"COMP-29875534ea059f1d","baseline_parent_code":"SET028","secondary_class":"SC-L8-e260aa47afe769d8","category":{"id":"SC-L8-e260aa47afe769d8","stage":"L8","label_zh":"Transaction consideration and bill settlement","definition_zh":"Determine, account for, or settle the monetary consideration and accounts of resource transactions, including amounts, settlement rates, and overall receipts and payments; charges, deviation adjustments, and pass-through arrangements within composite accounts are retained by source.","main_identity_axes":["Economic purpose: discharge the monetary-consideration obligation of a resource transaction","Primary objects: transaction sums, bills, net receipts and payments, or the basis for their settlement","Responsibility relation: receipt and payment by the transaction participants, which may include platform collection and pass-through"],"inclusion_criteria":["It principally specifies the payment, receipt, pricing basis, or bill settlement corresponding to a resource transaction.","Product differences such as electrical energy, storage use, and demand reduction may serve as source branches.","Composite units centered on transaction accounts are included as a whole, retaining the charge, deviation, and overall-net accounting bases."],"exclusion_criteria":["It only specifies the monetary meaning or accounting scope of an independent charge component.","Its primary function is collective cost allocation or distribution of cooperative gains.","It only specifies attribution of the operator's difference revenue or bearing of a funding shortfall.","It only describes that member payments are affected, and there is as yet no definite settlement relation or basis."],"supporting_unit_ids":["12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment","12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M02::settlement.payment","17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-M01::settlement.payment","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment","25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-a-M02-residual-balancing::settlement.payment","05-double_auction::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment","19-two_stage_battery_sharing::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment","21-dso_flexibility_optimization::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment"],"status":"agent_defined_structurally_validated"},"definition":{"definition_zh":"Account for participants' resource purchase costs and supply revenues; by source, retain the composition of production cost, transport cost, or demand value, forming a charge, a revenue, or a net amount, respectively.","identity_fields":["Economic purpose: account for the receipts and payments formed by resource transactions","Primary objects: purchase costs, supply revenues, and source-specified net amounts","Responsibility relation: the receipt-and-payment relation of a participant as resource buyer and as supplier"],"member_unit_ids":["27-multiple_microgrid_trading::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment","42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-a-M03::settlement.payment","42-data_center_space_time_flexibility::v141-a-M04::settlement.payment"]}},"canonical_pointer":"/nodes/42"});
